  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 1154 PM MDT Fri Aug 21 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nThe ridge axis associated with a high amplitude upper level ridge \nacross the Northern Rockies will begin shifting east of the area \nlate tonight and into the morning Saturday. This will eventually \nresult in a southwesterly flow aloft which will foster scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms this weekend ahead of a cold front. In the \nmeantime the remainder of the afternoon looks dry and warm. A few \nshowers or thunderstorms cannot be ruled out this evening along the \nContinental Divide in Southwest Montana, but those probabilities are \nlow. \n\nFlow aloft into tomorrow has a much more southerly component than \nwesterly, at least initially, which will aid in advecting in \nmonsoonal moisture. Combine this with ample flow and forcing \naloft from an approaching trough across the Pacific NW and \nscattered afternoon and evening thunderstorms will develop as a\nresult. There looks to be enough of a balance between drier \ninverted-V lower level profiles and ample moisture aloft to result\nin both a gusty wind and excessive rainfall risk Saturday \nafternoon and evening. Forcing associated with the approaching \nupper level trough from the Pacific NW will keep at least widely \nscattered showers and thunderstorms around through the overnight \nSaturday into Sunday morning. \n\nHeading into Sunday the core of the upper level disturbance passes \njust north of the region, which will foster both a thunderstorm \nthreat and fire weather threat. The magnitude and duration of each \nof these threats will be dictated by just how quickly an associated \ncold front moves through. \n\nHeading into next week Monday looks to be a slightly cooler day \nbehind a front, but temperatures quickly trend back warmer by \nTuesday. Persistent southwesterly flow aloft will keep at least \nisolated showers and thunderstorms in the forecast through next \nweek. -AM \n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nThunderstorms Saturday: \n\nThere is still a bit of a north-south discrepancy with respect to \nwhere showers and thunderstorms develop through the day. Given \nprecipitable water values near or in excess of an inch, wherever \nthese thunderstorms develop will have the risk for at least briefly \nheavy rain. Areas of greatest concern for localized flash flooding \nwill be in urban areas and over and near burn scars. The threat for \ngusty winds will also be around, with a few gusts in excess of 58 \nmph not out of the question. \n\nSundays fire weather and thunderstorm risk:\n\nA quicker front would increase the duration of winds and related \nfire weather concerns, while a slower front would allow for greater \ninstability to form and result in a greater thunderstorm risk. The \nkinematic environment for thunderstorms ahead of the front would be \nsufficient for a few organized thunderstorms, including a supercell \nor two, capable of gusty winds and hail. -AM",
